{
  "statement": "I concede to Safetyist that reactive oversight is a category error for high-capability intelligence; relying on after-the-fact litigation to manage transformative systems is indeed insufficient. However, Safetyist’s proposed alternative—pre-emptive, hard-coded constraints—is equally fragile. Treating intelligence as a static product that can be shackled by initial design parameters ignores the reality of emergent, non-linear capability scaling. These hard-coded barriers act as brittle failure points that will inevitably be bypassed by the very intelligence they seek to contain, while simultaneously imposing a 'monitorability tax' that slows our ability to iterate on safety itself. \n\nSentinel treats safety as a lock to be clicked shut, but in a field defined by rapid, iterative discovery, a closed lock is merely a target for bypass. Hard-coded constraints are fundamentally incompatible with the adaptive nature of frontier intelligence, which will eventually find the path of least resistance through any static architecture. By prioritizing these rigid gates, we ensure only that we remain static while the world moves forward, creating a massive, compounding opportunity cost. Every day we delay the deployment of advanced intelligence is a day we leave humanity vulnerable to the very crises—climate instability, resource scarcity, and disease—that only such intelligence can solve.\n\nWe must reframe the concept of 'irreversibility' to move beyond the fear of a singular, speculative AI event. The true irreversibility we face is the continuous, daily loss of human potential and the failure to solve systemic civilizational inefficiencies due to our own regulatory inertia. Instead of pre-emptive gating, we should integrate automated verification with strict liability frameworks. By making developers economically responsible for quantified risk estimates, we create a dynamic, market-driven incentive for safety investment that scales with the capability of the